# Task list

Give a longer setup process clear tasks and visible progress.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use a task list when people may complete work in several sessions or in an order they choose. Break complex tasks into meaningful sections and preserve completed work.

## When not to use it

Do not add it to a simple two-step preference change. It is not a list of saved answers or a live device checklist; use a summary list for answers and ordinary status presentation for readings.

## Home Assistant adaptation

A dashboard setup may include choosing rooms, reviewing labels and checking the finished view. Mark a task completed only when its required work is saved. Explain dependencies instead of presenting an unexplained disabled link. Keep any final activation separate from progress labels.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use one clear link per task, associate its status and keep additional hints short. Do not put separate links inside a linked task row. Ensure “Cannot start yet” is readable without colour and explain which prerequisite is missing.

